b/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497348 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497348 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497348 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?\n\n$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497348 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32e7120e-ecd0-54a1-8649-0fea9e5b153d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497348 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by ${x}^{2}$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{3}{x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{3}{x}W(x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497358 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497358 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497358 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497358 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+(1+x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(2)=5.$ What is $W(6)?$\n\n$W(6)=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497358 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53cebc23-dff1-56fe-a57a-9a0d9ca25a96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497358 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-3\\cdot 2} \\\\ 5 & =C{e}^{-6} \\\\ C & =5{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=5{e}^{6}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(6) & =5{e}^{6}{e}^{-3\\cdot 6}=5{e}^{-12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497362 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497362 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497362 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497362 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-4){y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?\n\n$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497362 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64a84975-134c-59e7-b3de-faa7ec811813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497362 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =(4-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))W(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497373 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497373 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497373 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497373 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n(x+1){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?\n\n$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497373 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "753b45d7-da51-5748-9817-073e29597daf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497373 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x+1$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{2x}{x+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{x+1}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2x}{x+1}W(x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497400 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497400 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497400 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497400 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497400 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497400 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfi