igned}{W}^{′} & =-W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-2} \\\\ 5 & =C{e}^{-2} \\\\ C & =5{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=5{e}^{2}{e}^{-x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497417 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497417 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497417 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497417 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}{y}^{′}+(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=10.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497417 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa87e239-f514-5ab3-af2e-43a62163086f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497417 #edb/ref 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}({x}^{2}+1)^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we 